WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.040
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.269
This is going to be a relatively short webinar
today.

00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:11.990
The introductory slide there gives you standard
information.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:16.431
Maybe one of the most important things is,
feel free to ask questions in the question

00:00:16.431 --> 00:00:19.910
pod during the webinar as you think of those
questions.

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:25.170
We always have the questions and answers in
a Materials link on the courses and webinars

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.040
page in archived webinars.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.630
Just search NCBI courses and webinars and
you will find the page.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:36.770
Within a few days, this webinar, which is
being recorded, will be on our YouTube channel

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:38.280
along with the others.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:43.100
Please feel free to email me directly if you
have questions afterward at matten@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:52.250
You can also send general BLAST questions
to blast-help@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov . Sitting here

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:57.540
with me today is Christiam Camacho and Tom
Madden, developers from the BLAST group.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.390
You will get some very expert answers to your
questions.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.990
Peter Cooper is on the question pod answering
questions during the webinar.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:10.890
Let me go ahead and give you the take home
message for today.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:17.479
The take home message is we highly encourage
you to upgrade to version 2.5.0 before November

00:01:17.479 --> 00:01:19.070
9.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.680
The next slide will tell you what will happen
on November 9.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:30.410
The first of the topics today has to do with
a NCBI-wide switch to HTTPS from standard

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:35.860
HTTP, but this will particularly affect standalone
BLAST in a couple of ways that I'll show you

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:37.320
on the next slide.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.940
This change will take effect for APIs that
deal with blast.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.240
The general webpages have already made the
change.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.570
We will also talk about another change which
is the phasing out of G.I.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:56.230
numbers and how using the accession.version
will impact standalone blast.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:02.220
We will very briefly talk touch on a couple
of improvements that came out in this release.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:10.479
So, what's going to break on Nov 9 if you
are still using version 2.4 .0 or earlier?

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.820
Primarily the remote option will not work.

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.790
If you send searches to our servers, that
will fail to work unless you have upgraded

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:27.410
to 2.5.0 . Another function, some of you may
or may not be using, but it is very handy,

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.270
and that is a prefetch function.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:36.710
In the past, you could only use that with
the remote option, but in this version, it

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:37.710
has been improved.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.160
You can use it without the remote option.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:47.290
What happens is you input a query file which
contains identifiers, either GIs or accessions,

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:52.959
and the programs will fetch those IDs from
NCBI.

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:59.099
You will not be able to use these two functions
if you have not upgraded before November 9.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:06.300
Having said that, we plan to have a test this
coming Thursday, October 13 from 10 AM until

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:07.599
11 AM Eastern time.

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:13.470
In other words, try running version 2.4.0
and it should not work during that time but

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.040
2.5.0 should.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.810
We may very well have other tests before the
ninth.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.349
We probably will.

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:24.920
Look on our webpages for an announcement for
those tests.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:30.290
Hopefully we will get an announcement out
in the blast-announce email list for those

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.989
other upcoming tests.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:37.290
The second major change has to do with phasing
out GIs.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:41.820
I have a couple of links in the last slides
where you can get more general information

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.739
about both the HTTPS change and the phase
out of gi's.

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:52.260
In terms of standalone BLAST , what will happen
is you will be seeing more and more the new

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.310
style of definition lines versus the old-style.

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:01.709
In the old-style, the definition lines start
with gi and then the pipe symbol and then

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:03.150
the number and so on.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:09.989
We are moving now to just what we call bare
accession.version.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:16.299
Those of you who are downloading our FASTA
databases from the BLAST ftp site have already

00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:17.690
noticed this.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:23.820
We've have already switched over to the accession.version
definition line.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.199
There are other places where this will become
more apparent in standalone BLAST.

00:04:28.199 --> 00:04:34.660
For example in blastdbdmc, if you retrieve
sequences from our pre-formatted databases,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:41.249
that is, those one directory up from FASTA,
the default setting for returning FASTA will

00:04:41.249 --> 00:04:46.089
spit out the new style format, accession.version.

00:04:46.089 --> 00:04:50.289
You may see in my example command line that
I am using an accession.

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:58.889
You can still use gi numbers to blastdbcmd,
but you will get back by default this accession.version

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:03.349
format.

00:05:03.349 --> 00:05:10.339
In Makeblastdb, it will accept both old- and
new-style definition lines.

00:05:10.339 --> 00:05:13.889
So you don't have to worry about changes things
just yet.

00:05:13.889 --> 00:05:20.730
It will also guess the source from the accession
alone if you are using -parse_seqids; and

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:25.999
I assume that applies to our pre-formatted
databases.

00:05:25.999 --> 00:05:32.360
Also, many of you use the -gilist option to
create a custom database for an organism or

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.090
taxonomic group.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:42.529
That switch is still available and will remain
available, but we also have a -seqidlist option.

00:05:42.529 --> 00:05:44.539
That -seqidlist option will take accession.versioins.

00:05:44.539 --> 00:05:50.580
Right now, -seqidlist is kind of slow with
large lists, but it should be improved in

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.589
future releases.

00:05:51.589 --> 00:05:56.800
We hope by the next release, but if not, the
one after tha.

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:03.620
The next release by the way is 2.6.0 and as
of today, that is scheduled to come out either

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:06.869
in December of this year or early next year.

00:06:06.869 --> 00:06:09.300
Okay, a couple other changes.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:15.279
In the default output formats, for those of
you using the tabular output formats, you

00:06:15.279 --> 00:06:22.159
are now going to get by default query accession,
sequence accession as shown here.

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:25.699
In the next release, you will be getting accession.version.

00:06:25.699 --> 00:06:33.270
You will not be getting back by default the
gi style definition lines.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:35.169
Those are the two major changes.

00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:41.569
One of the improvements we have made is to
offer another output format option and that

00:06:41.569 --> 00:06:43.460
is output format option 18.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:47.759
It will now give you a taxonomy organism report.

00:06:47.759 --> 00:06:51.270
If you are familiar with using this on the
web, it is similar in look to that.

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:56.879
It very nicely organizes the output by organism.

00:06:56.879 --> 00:07:02.499
And finally in terms of improvement, we now
have included composition-based statistics

00:07:02.499 --> 00:07:03.629
when you use RPSTBLASTN.

00:07:03.629 --> 00:07:09.830
If you are not familiar with this program,
it allows you to input a nucleotide sequence,

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:15.719
then does the translations and searches the
conserved domains database.

00:07:15.719 --> 00:07:19.259
You get back conserved domains.

00:07:19.259 --> 00:07:26.429
RPSBLAST allows you to input a protein sequence
and see what conserved remains are there.

00:07:26.429 --> 00:07:29.619
RPSTBLASTN allows you to input a nucleotide
sequence.

00:07:29.619 --> 00:07:33.759
I promised a couple of links.

00:07:33.759 --> 00:07:40.580
You can go to our news page to find out about
the general gi number phase out.

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:46.319
Go to our home page in the Develop section
and the API section and you can read about

00:07:46.319 --> 00:07:49.839
the switch to HTTPS.

00:07:49.839 --> 00:07:54.370
For a full discussion of the release notes,
go to the NCBI bookshelf.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:08:01.779
You can just search NCBI bookshelf blast,
and you'll get these release notes.

00:08:01.779 --> 00:08:04.810
I am going to open this up for any questions.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:10.410
I am going to check out our chat pod, here,
to see if we've had any questions come in.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:11.410
Okay.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:15.000
We are not seeing any questions at the moment.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.360
Thank you very much and we will see you at
the next webinar.

